  "query_preview": "..\nSynoptic Setup: \n\nSurface map is showing a low over northern Canada with a decent\npressure gradient over Montana this afternoon. This has lead to an\nincrease in W to SW winds that have bumped temps across the CWA above\nnormal with highs near 70 north to the upper 70s south.\n\nChinook arch clouds were quite thick this morning across the\nregion and are expected to gradually decrease as a moisture stream\nfrom the northern Pacific pushes south slightly.\n\nWednesday into Friday: The jet stream near the US/Canadian border\nwill go from a zonal position to more of a W-NW orientation.\nDisturbances in this fast flow could kick off a few showers\nThursday and Friday. Temps will be quite cool Thursday and should\nbe the coolest day in this forecast period.\n\nBeyond Friday: Surface high pressure over the Canadian prairies \nlooks to be in place this weekend with a NW upper flow. This will\nkeep daytime temps slightly below normal.\n\nEarly next week, an upper ridge looks to move in from the west\nwhile a surface low over NW Canada helps to push a milder Pacific\nairmass into the area for above normal temps.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\nThe chinook arch over the area today signaled that these will be \nan occasional weather feature to watch until next Spring. Models \ndo not prog these very well and public perception may become \nsoured with our optimistic forecasts when these occur.\n\nTherefore, blended Sky grids with NAM12 and CONSMOS. Otherwise,\ngoing NBM looked good. TFJ",
